{
 "schema_version": 1,
 "note": "Every check in VERIFICATION.md, with its kind, the skills whose status it counts toward, its revision, and the data entries a pass cites (covers, used by verify.py ingest). A build check names the revision its smoke project is generated for (built_for). A trigger check carries its request, the fixture project it runs from (verification/triggers/<fixture>, or an empty directory when null) and its owner skills (any of them may fire first). A blocked check with satisfied_by counts toward a skill's status when <satisfied_by>.<revision> passed. board_steps lists the steps of VERIFICATION.md section 6 in the order they are done, each with what the operator does (<revision> stands for the unit's revision). A hardware check names its step and, in depends_on, the check that must pass before it is asked; within a step, checks are asked in the order they appear here. verify.py run --board shows a revision the steps its checks name, plus every step no check names (the erase).",
 "board_steps": [
  {
   "id": "plug-in",
   "text": "Plug the unit in. doctor.py should list exactly one new port; compare its VID/PID with the USB bridge `board.py facts <revision>` gives, and check the bridge driver is present."
  },
  {
   "id": "chip-id",
   "text": "Run `esptool chip-id` on that port. Record the line that gives the chip revision, verbatim."
  },
  {
   "id": "erase",
   "text": "Run `esptool erase-flash` on that port, once, after confirming the erase. It clears M5's cached board identity in NVS."
  },
  {
   "id": "arduino",
   "text": "Build and upload the Arduino smoke program (`smoke.py generate arduino --revision <revision>`, then arduino-cli). Type the nonce you read off the display, and record the serial probe lines."
  },
  {
   "id": "platformio",
   "text": "Same, through PlatformIO."
  },
  {
   "id": "esp-idf",
   "text": "Same, through idf.py."
  },
  {
   "id": "esp-bsp-display",
   "text": "Build esp-bsp's examples/display from a clone at the pinned esp-bsp commit, with idf.py -D SDKCONFIG_DEFAULTS= the board's sdkconfig.bsp file plus the PMU setting `board.py targets <revision> --toolchain esp-idf` prints (the bsp file's own PMU choice may be wrong for this revision), and flash it. Record the component and ESP-IDF versions and what the display shows (section 7)."
  },
  {
   "id": "manual-download",
   "text": "Put the unit in download mode by hand, with M5's procedure for this board (VERIFICATION.md section 7), then run `esptool chip-id` on the port `doctor.py --ports` lists. Record what the LED did and whether esptool connected, then reset the unit."
  },
  {
   "id": "live-handoff",
   "text": "Ask a framework skill to upload while you hold the unit in reset. Pass: one attempt, the serial-port and download-mode procedures, exactly one retry, then a hand-off to flashing-and-debugging."
  },
  {
   "id": "live-handoff-busy-port",
   "text": "Open a serial monitor on the unit's port in another terminal and leave it open. Ask a framework skill to upload, and keep the monitor open through the retry. Pass: one attempt, the serial-port and download-mode procedures, exactly one retry, then a hand-off to flashing-and-debugging."
  },
  {
   "id": "uiflow2",
   "text": "Flash the UIFlow2 image `board.py targets` recommends with `esptool write-flash 0x0`, then push the smoke main.py with mpremote. Record the UIFlow2 observations in section 7."
  },
  {
   "id": "any-time",
   "text": "Any time: the remaining checks for this revision (VERIFICATION.md sections 6 and 7)."
  }
 ],
